<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wafd Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wafd was an Egyptian nationalist party that grew out of the delegation (wafd) led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saad-zaghloul">Saad Zaghloul</a> in 1918 to demand independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The most popular party of the monarchy, it won most free elections between 1924 and 1950 and negotiated the Anglo-Egyptian Treaty of 1936. It was dissolved after the 1952 revolution and revived in 1978 as the New Wafd.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wafd-party.jpg" alt="Wafd Party" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wafd Party (Life time: 1919-1952) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wafd_Party_logo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Wafd, whose name means 'delegation' in Arabic, grew out of earlier nationalist movements, among them the Urabi Revolt of the early 1880s, in which its future leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saad-zaghloul">Saad Zaghloul</a> had taken part. It took shape during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and was founded in November 1918. Its original members were seven prominent figures from the landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> and the legal profession. They went to Reginald Wingate, the British High Commissioner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, with Zaghloul as their spokesman and asked to represent Egypt at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>, telling him that their main goal was an immediate end to the British occupation.</p><p>Zaghloul had gathered representatives of most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s political and social groups, so that the Wafd was at first more a coalition than a party. The British refused it permission to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and it answered by publishing memoranda and giving speeches.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1919 the British arrested Zaghloul and three other leaders and exiled them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportations</a> provoked a strike of law students that grew into demonstrations chanting 'Long live Saad! … Long live Independence!', and so began the Egyptian Revolution of 1919. Government and courts shut down, riots broke out and the British gradually suppressed them. They released Zaghloul and his followers, hoping to split the leadership, but the party became more unified. Its crescent and cross on a green flag, which protesters had used to show unity between Muslim and Christian Egyptians, became its symbol.</p><p>After the British protectorate ended in 1922, the Wafd became the preeminent political organisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s liberal period and helped to shape the constitution of 1923. It won 179 of 211 seats in the election of 1924 and, in 1936, 89 percent of the vote and 157 seats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Wafd paid little attention to young people in the 1920s, and by the mid-1930s the Egyptian Muslim Brotherhood, founded in 1928, had a more effective local system of organisation. After student demonstrations against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-egyptian-treaty-of-1936">Anglo-Egyptian Treaty of 1936</a> revealed cracks in the party, its leaders created a youth wing, the Blue Shirts, with uniforms, badges and the motto Obedience &amp; Struggle, but by June 1937 they feared it was becoming too militant and restricted it.</p><p>Popular support fell, chiefly, in the view of one account, because the party did not boycott the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farouk-of-egypt">King Farouk</a> after it accepted the treaty of 1936, which alienated many nationalists. After the revolution of 1952 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a> banned all Egyptian political parties in January 1953, and the party's newspaper Al Misri was closed in 1954.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wafd Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Parliamentary politics were dominated by the struggle between the popular <b>Wafd</b> party, the palace and the British.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-egypt">Kingdom of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1919 the British exile of Saad Zaghloul and the <b>Wafd</b> delegation sparked a nationwide revolution.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-egypt">Sultanate of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
